      "side": 1,
      "message": "This could be flattened using an actual error type:\n```\nenum PteModificationError {\n    Unavailable,\n    NotInitialized,\n    InternalError(MemoryTrackerError),\n}\n\nimpl Display for PteModificationError {\n    // ....\n}\n\nimpl From\u003cMemoryTrackerError\u003e for PteModificationError {\n    fn from(e) -\u003e ... {\n        Self::InternalError(e)\n    }\n}\n```\nthen `?` makes your code much easier to follow:\n```\nfn modify_pte_range(...) -\u003e Result\u003c(), PteModificationError\u003e {\n    let mut locked \u003d MEMORY.try_lock().ok_or(PteModificationError::Unavailable)?;\n    let memory \u003d locked.as_mut().ok_or(PteModificationError::NotInitialized)?;\n\n    memory.modify_range(va_range, f)\n}\n```\nand centralize logging at the call-site:\n```\nmatch modify_pte_range(\u0026(far..far + 1), \u0026handle_flagged_page_fault) {\n    Ok(()) \u003d\u003e return,\n    Err(e) if !is_uart \u003d\u003e eprintln!(\"{e}\"),\n    _ \u003d\u003e (),\n}\n```\n(BTW, I don\u0027t think `eprintln!` can safely be used from the handler, but might be wrong)",
